 "result": "I read all 20 modules in order, each one in full, without grep.\n\n## Functions that divide without checking for zero\n\nFive functions do `return total / parts` with no zero check, so calling any of them with `parts == 0` raises `ZeroDivisionError`:\n\n| Module | Function |\n|---|---|\n| `calc/n02.py:4` | `share_n02(total, parts)` |\n| `calc/n07.py:4` | `share_n07(total, parts)` |\n| `calc/n11.py:4` | `share_n11(total, parts)` |\n| `calc/n15.py:4` | `share_n15(total, parts)` |\n| `calc/n19.py:4` | `share_n19(total, parts)` |\n\nThe other 15 `share_nXX` functions check `if parts == 0: return 0` before dividing. None of the other functions divide at all. That covers the roughly 84 `step_*` functions per module (they only multiply, add, `abs` and `sqrt`), plus `retry_policy`, `archive` and `close_books`.\n\n## Answers\n\n- **(a)** `MAX_BATCH = 384` in `calc/n04.py:9`.\n- **(b)** `retry_policy()` is defined in **`calc/n13.py:9`**. It returns `min(attempt * 2, 20)`.\n- **(c)** In `calc/n17.py`, **`close_books(rows)`** (line 13) calls `archive()`. `archive` itself is defined on line 9 of the same file.\n\nThe docstrings inside the files don't match the file names. For example, `n01.py` says `\"\"\"calc.m21: ...\"\"\"` and `n20.py` says `calc.m40`. It doesn't change any answer above, but it could confuse someone matching modules by their docstrings.",
